  "summary": "Carved roof-spear spirits topping Kanak chiefs' houses — now the national symbol of New Caledonia.",
  "description": "The flèche faîtière crowns a Kanak great-house ridgepole: a carved central ancestor figure flanked by flaring horns — one guarding the lineage, one the clan's guests — so the ancestor looks down from the apex, watching speech and keeping order in the chief's house. Carved of houp hardwood, the spires were suppressed under missionary and colonial rule and reborn as New Caledonia's national emblem: the Tjibaou Cultural Centre and public monuments repeat their silhouette, and carvers renew the form for customary ceremonies.",
